{"name":"Idol","hex":"#645a8b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"idol","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A graven image or representation of anything that is revered, or believed to convey spiritual power.","A cultural icon, or especially popular person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/idol"}],"entries":[{"title":"Idolatry","blocks":[{"text":"Idolatry is the worship of an idol as though it were a deity. In this context, the term idol, usually pejorative in English, is an imagen or representation of a god used as an object of worship. The term is often associated with Abrahamic religions, where idolatry connotes the worship of something or someone other than the Abrahamic God."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Worshiping_the_golden_calf.jpg/330px-Worshiping_the_golden_calf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idolatry","wikidata":"Q840665"},{"title":"Teen idol","blocks":[{"text":"A teen idol is a celebrity with a large teenage fan base. Teen idols are generally young but are not necessarily teenagers themselves. An idol's popularity may be limited to teens, or may extend to all age groups."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Beatles_ad_1965_just_the_beatles_crop.jpg/330px-Beatles_ad_1965_just_the_beatles_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teen_idol","wikidata":"Q1034298"},{"title":"Idol (franchise)","blocks":[{"text":"Idol, commonly known as Idols, is a reality television singing competition format created by British television producer Simon Fuller and developed by Fremantle. The format began in 2001 with the British television series Pop Idol; its first adaptation was the South African series Idols in 2002. It has since become the world's most widely watched television franchise, as well as one of the most successful entertainment formats, adapted in over 56 regions around the world, with its various versions broadcast to 150 countries with a worldwide audience of roughly 3.2 billion people. The franchise has generated more than $2.5 billion in revenue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/dd/Idols_2013_logo.png/330px-Idols_2013_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idol_(franchise)","wikidata":"Q4166962"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Decalogue_parchment_by_Jekuthiel_Sofer_1768.jpg/330px-Decalogue_parchment_by_Jekuthiel_Sofer_1768.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Decalogue_parchment_by_Jekuthiel_Sofer_1768.jpg/330px-Decalogue_parchment_by_Jekuthiel_Sofer_1768.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 1768 synagogue parchment with the Ten Commandments by Jekuthiel Sofer. Among other things, it prohibits idolatry.[32]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADecalogue_parchment_by_Jekuthiel_Sofer_1768.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/William_Blake_-_Moses_Indignant_at_the_Golden_Calf.jpg/330px-William_Blake_-_Moses_Indignant_at_the_Golden_Calf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/William_Blake_-_Moses_Indignant_at_the_Golden_Calf.jpg/330px-William_Blake_-_Moses_Indignant_at_the_Golden_Calf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Moses Indignant at the Golden Calf, painting by William Blake, 1799–1800","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWilliam_Blake_-_Moses_Indignant_at_the_Golden_Calf.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/BMVB_